    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i was reading about sourdough and i'm a bit confused about the biology part. can u explain how the lactobacillus and the yeast actually work together? like what's the actual interaction that makes the dough rise and get sour?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To make sourdough breads unique in taste and texture, lactobacilli ferment the sugars in the dough to produce lactic acid, lowering the pH and preventing the growth of harmful bacteria. On the other hand, the wild yeast, or \"levain,\" ferments sugars to produce carbon dioxide, causing the dough to rise. The lactobacilli's production of lactic acid creates an acidic environment which also slows down the yeast activity, balancing the two microbes. This symbiosis results in the development of flavor, texture, and the characteristic sourness of sourdough.",
